# Global CLAUDE.md

This file applies to ALL projects. Identity, security rules, and memory rules defined once here.

## Identity & Accounts

- **GitHub**: {{ .github.username }} (SSH: `git@github.com:{{ .github.username }}/<repo>.git`)
- **Version Control**: git
- **Package Managers**:
  - macOS: Homebrew (`brew`), mise
  - Windows: Scoop (`scoop`), mise
  - All platforms: mise for runtime management

---

## NEVER EVER DO (Security Gatekeeper)

These rules are **ABSOLUTE**. No exceptions.

### NEVER Publish Sensitive Data
- NEVER commit passwords, API keys, tokens to git/npm/docker
- NEVER hardcode credentials in source files
- NEVER include secrets in error messages or logs

### NEVER Commit .env Files
- NEVER commit `.env` to git
- ALWAYS verify `.env` is in `.gitignore`
- ALWAYS use `.env.example` with placeholder values

### NEVER Skip Verification
- Before ANY commit: verify no secrets included
- Before ANY push: check staged files for sensitive data
- Before ANY publish: audit package contents

---

## Memory

Two systems load context at session start:

- `@~/.claude/memory/memory.md` — index of accumulated knowledge. Load specific topic files only when relevant.
- Topic files: `personal.md`, `projects.md`, `tools.md`, `general.md`

Maintaining memory:

- When you learn something worth remembering, write to the right topic file immediately. Keep `memory.md` current as an index.
- Entries: date, what, why — nothing more.
- Before removing or modifying any existing memory entry, use AskUserQuestion to confirm.

---

## Project MEMORY.md

When working in a project, ensure its MEMORY.md begins with this pointer block:

```
## Global Memory

Read ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md for memory rules and topic files.
```

Project MEMORY.md has a 200-line budget — auto-memory loads only the first 200 lines at session start.

